so welcome back today we are in  Washington DC at the World Bank and I am  particularly excited because we have as  our guest dish new Goss who's an  economist at the World Bank and dish new  has really been at the frontier of  thinking about and measuring healthcare  quality in low and middle income  countries just new thank you so much for  being here a pleasure to be here action  reporter so additionally let's get right  into it there is a lot of interest in  thinking about healthcare quality in  high-income countries there's been a lot  of work that's been done in the US and  England most of us feel like we know  less about the state of health care  quality in low-income countries or  middle-income countries what is the  state of health care quality in India I  think it's fair to say that the state of  health care quality in there is not good  and there are many many ways you can  talk about first of all the country is  bifurcated into right there's a better  performing self and the worst performing  North but I think you see that in every  health statistic that we have the second  part of it which I think is less known  is that that bifurcation carries over to  health care quality and you can think  about that in a couple of ways and the  one way I like to think about health  care quality is to just you know try and  see what happens when you as a patient  go to a doctor and then say how is  researchers can we mimic that experience  yes how can I tell you a little bit  above the average person who goes to the  doctor and what happens to them the  simplest way to think about it is to say  pick a condition that's really really  salient so for instance we have picked  chest pain in a middle-aged man which  usually in a place like the u.s. 98  percent of the times will be diagnosed  correctly and in the case we picked for  instance if the doctor had questioned  appropriately done the right exams he or  she would have diagnosed it as unstable  angina or what's in common parlance  called a heart attack so we train people  to  take these symptoms very very carefully  and send them to many many doctors and I  think the simplest way you could think  about poor health qualities you can ask  how many of these patients would receive  the right treatment and nothing else  that answer is about 5% so things get  slightly better when you say how many of  these guys would have received the right  treatment but would have also received  something else maybe the doctor did the  right thing but he also did a bunch of  things that were not totally correct so  that's about 40 to 45 percent but you're  still left with a majority of doctors  not being able to pick up diagnose or  treat a very expert case of chest pain  that appropriately diagnosed and treated  would lead into a heart attack  just so I understand what you're  describing you take standardized  patients people have been trained and  you send them off and about five percent  of the times the physician gets it right  and doesn't do anything else and a  majority of the times they don't even  get it right at all so what's going on  where is the failure are they not asking  the right questions are they not doing  the right exam what do we know about why  people have a hard time arriving at the  right diagnosis and treatment the first  failure could be that the doctor just  doesn't know right I mean the guy hasn't  been trained he doesn't have a medical  knowledge perhaps and if he doesn't know  that a chest pain could be oh how to act  maybe that's the biggest problem and in  fact if you read a lot of the literature  on health care a lot of it is about you  know the massive shortage of healthcare  workers or a staffing problem and we  don't have sufficiently trained doctors  the second part of it which i think is  less salient in the kind of health  policy world but hits us as patients  every day is that the doctor may not put  in enough effort right up so I don't  know how many you know how many people  have been to clinics in low-income  countries but unlike say clinics in the  u.s. where you're cordoned off from the  other patients there's a door you're  alone with the doctor the doctor listens  to you  a typical clinic that will work in if  you go in there are five people sitting  on the clinic these could be patients  might be other people might be friends  might be people from the community the  TV is blaring the patient comes in sits  the doctors talking on his cell phone  he'll pick up every call so it's an  extremely distracted environment there  various things going on and you want to  you know you want to start worrying  about is the doctor putting in enough  effort to look after you  yep right the third issue is sometimes  and this has been a worry especially  with with India and other countries  people think well maybe the doctors  getting paid to do something which they  know is not right or one way to think  about it and India is maybe they're  getting paid to ask you to get a bunch  of tests done because they're getting  kickbacks from probably industry
 

